From 68e8aa86268b44d7c26fd974043d890233acf1a8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 14:10:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Victory screen, two crash fixes, notification jump, paging
 fix, slower speeds

Six things Bob reported after finishing a mission.

1. Winning crashed. Interface.update indexes msg_category by MessageType, and
   that table stops at call_to_stock (19) while game_won and game_lost are 20
   and 21 - "Variable Index [20] out of range [20]" the moment the game was won.
   The table gains both, in category 0, which the default config has on and no
   message-count setting turns off. The two lookups now go through
   interface_msg_category_of(), which falls back to category 0 rather than
   throwing, so the next message kind added cannot repeat this.

2. flag_prio[-1]. Flag.schedule_known_dest_cb_ reads the type of the slot that
   other_end_dir already names, and that slot can be EMPTY: a slot scheduled
   through the not-yet-scheduled branch never gets its `dir` set, so Flag.update
   keeps offering it and it can end up named by two directions at once. The
   transporter that collects it calls prioritize_pickup for its own direction
   only, leaving the other still flagged as scheduled and pointing at a slot
   whose type is now none. Freeserf has the same hole - there it is a silent
   out-of-bounds read, which is why it never shows. An empty slot is now treated
   as no competition, so whatever is waiting takes the schedule and overwrites
   the stale pointer, which heals it.

3. The end of the game now has a screen. PopupType.game_end_box was an enum
   entry with nothing behind it; it draws the result, the faces of everyone who
   was in the game, and two choices - carry on with the finished world, or back
   to the start screen. Interface.update opens it once, tracked by
   Game.game_over_shown so it survives save/load and never reopens.

   Faces are 48x72 with a ~32x64 portrait inset, so two fit side by side and
   three do not; at three the colour blocks drop to 40 wide and tile edge to
   edge while the portraits still stand clear.

   Winning a mission is now recorded. Game.mission_index is set by GameInitBox
   when it starts one (-1 for a custom game, which has nothing to tick) and
   serialises, so a mission finished from a save still counts. The set of
   completed missions lives in settlers_progress.json, written the moment a
   mission is won rather than at shutdown, and the mission selector shows DONE
   beside one already beaten.

4. Clicking a notification now goes to the place it is telling you about and
   answers with Sfx.accepted. Before, the jump happened once in open_message and
   the box was a dead end - by the time you had read it and clicked it away, you
   had lost the spot. A message with no position (the since-save reminders)
   closes with the ordinary click sound, so a dead click never sounds live.

5. The build popup vanished while paging. Two clicks on the flip button inside
   MOUSE_TIME_SENSITIVITY (600 ms) make the second one a click AND a double
   click; the click turned the page, and the double click fell through - the
   default GuiObject handler returns false, which gui_handle_event reads as
   "this float did not want it" and hands down to the viewport, where
   fast_building_click acted on the map behind the popup. PopupBox, PanelBar and
   NotificationBox now swallow double clicks. Floats inside the popup that do
   use them, like the save-file list, are offered the event first and are
   unaffected.

6. Speed steps halved, 20/50/100 -> 10/25/50. Normal play is deliberately NOT
   touched: step 1 stays at DEFAULT_GAME_SPEED, which is Freeserf's value and
   therefore the Amiga's pace.

